## English

**Fangame Launcher** is an application for desktop (Windows, Linux and macOS) and Android for downloading and playing fan games.

### Data it uses

- **Your games' save files.** If you connect Google Drive, the launcher copies those saves to your Google Drive account and restores them on other devices.
- **Download counter.** When a download or update finishes and its integrity is verified, the launcher sends our server an event with: a random event identifier, the game, the version, the build platform, the launcher's operating system, the launcher version, the file's SHA-256 hash and whether it is a download or an update. The server adds the date. **That record contains no account, name, device, hardware, IP address or user-agent**, and the identifier is not derived from you or your device. It is used only to show how many times each game was downloaded. To limit abuse, the server briefly uses your IP address in a volatile cache (as a salted hash that expires after 60 seconds); it is not stored in the counter database. The hosting infrastructure may keep technical access logs according to its own configuration.
- **Crash reports.** Desktop launcher only (Windows, Linux and macOS), not Android. When a catalogue game closes because of an error (its `errorlog.txt` appears or changes or, on Windows and Linux, it exits with an error code), the launcher asks whether to send a report to the game's creator. Nothing is sent unless you press “Send”. You can choose “Don't ask again for this game”. That choice is stored only on your device, not on the server. The report contains: the game, its version and the SHA-256 hash of the installed file, the system (Windows, Linux or macOS), how it ran (native, Proton, Wine or Whisky), the type of failure, the text of `errorlog.txt` or, if there is none, the exit code and the last 100 lines of the game's console output, the launcher version and an optional comment of up to 300 characters, filtered for inappropriate terms. Before sending, personal paths (user folder, system username, game folder and the Proton or Wine prefix) are replaced with placeholders. You can see the exact content before sending it. The account, saves and device identifier are not sent. The server does not store your IP address; it uses it only to limit submissions. The game's team sees the reports in the portal, grouped by error, version and system. Reports are deleted after 90 days. Administrators' review installs do not send reports.
- **Public game credits.** Maker accounts can provide the names of people or teams they want to credit; those names appear on game pages and in the catalogue. The owner account's username is used as a fallback credit only when no authors are provided.
- **Followed games and availability notices.** When you are signed in, the backend stores the game identifiers you follow and the date you started following each one in your account. The launcher also keeps a local copy so it works offline; without an account, the list and dates stay on your device. When you see a notice that a public milestone (demo, public beta or full game) was first published after you started following it, the game, milestone and time shown are saved to your account so the notice is not repeated on your other devices. Private betas do not generate these notices. Makers see only an aggregate follower count, without names or account identities. Signing out separates the local copy from the account; data already synced remains associated with that account.
- **Player account.** Creating an account is optional. When you create one, you provide a username, email address and password. To sign in, the launcher sends your username and password to the backend over HTTPS for authentication. The password is not stored in plain text. The backend links your account to private beta games for which the team has granted you access. The launcher stores a session token in your operating system's secure credential store; the server stores only its hash. The token expires after 30 days. When you sign out, the launcher attempts to revoke it on the server and always deletes the local copy; if there is no connection, the server token remains valid until it expires. To limit login attempts, the backend temporarily keeps a salted hash of the IP address in a volatile cache for one minute. When you create an account in the launcher, the backend emails a temporary six-digit code to the address provided. Only a hash of the code is stored; it expires after 10 minutes and allows up to five attempts. After verification, the email is marked as verified and the launcher signs you in automatically.
- **Account deletion.** You can request deletion from Account in the launcher or portal by confirming with your password (or username if the account has no password). The request revokes tokens and web sessions, pauses access and makes the profile unavailable. An administrator can approve deletion from 14 days after the request; it is not automatic. You can sign in during that period and keep the account. If you make games, each one must first be transferred to another team member or retired. Once completed, the account and its related data are deleted, including the profile, follows, recommendations and earned avatars. Audit records retain numeric IDs; the deletion record retains the username and account ID, and no deletion period is implemented for those records. Reports about your profile are deleted with it, but reports you submitted stay in the moderation queue without a link to your account. Your username is reserved for 90 days. An email is sent when the request is received and another when deletion is completed.
- **Replies to moderation emails.** If you reply to a moderation email about your game (for example, a rejection or a withdrawal), we store the text of your reply, the email address you wrote from and the release it refers to. Only the text is stored: attachments and the quoted text of the original email are not kept, and each reply allows up to 10,000 characters. Only the project's staff can read these replies, from the administration portal. Threads are deleted 90 days after being resolved or, if still open, 180 days after their last message. If you delete your account, your own replies and the threads not tied to a release are deleted, together with the staff replies they contain. Threads tied to a release keep only the staff's replies, without your name, until those periods end.

### Game data linked to your account

This data is sent only if you sign in with a player account. Without a session, the launcher keeps it on your device only.

- **Device identifier.** The launcher generates a random identifier the first time it needs one and stores it on your device. It is not derived from your device or its hardware. It is sent with playtime, achievements and completed games to tell your devices apart.
- **Playtime.** On desktop, when you play a catalogue game, the launcher sends the game, the start time, the seconds played and, from the launcher version that includes this feature, the installed version of the game. The server also records when each session starts and ends to measure the time itself. It is used to show your total time across your devices, to allow rating a game after 30 minutes of play, to verify achievements and for the aggregated statistics for makers.
- **Achievements.** Compatible games record achievements in a file inside their save folder. On desktop and Android, the launcher reads that file and sends the game, each achievement's identifier, its progress, the time recorded by the game and a random code per event. The server stores which achievements you have, when you unlocked them and your progress. To detect cheating, it marks as unverified the achievements that show signals such as a future date, a date before the account was created, many achievements at once, no playtime, an unlock outside a play session or the same event on another account. An unverified achievement does not count towards rarity. The owner of each game on the portal sees the unverified achievements of their game, with the account's username and the reasons, and can revoke them or reset that account's achievements in their game. The moderation team can do the same, and those actions are logged. Each achievement's rarity is shown only as a percentage of the accounts with activity in the game, without names, and only when there are at least 10.
- **Completed games.** When a compatible game reports that you finished it, the launcher sends the date, the playtime reported by the save if there is one, and whether the save or the launcher detected it. Only the first time per game is stored. Completed playthroughs are also used in the aggregated statistics for makers.
- **Ratings.** If you rate a game, one rating per account and game is stored; you can change or delete it. Only the average and the number of votes are shown publicly. Ratings are also used in the aggregated statistics for makers.
- **Library folders.** Your folder names, whether they are favourites and which games they contain are stored in your account so they look the same on your other devices.

Apart from what a game's owner sees about the unverified achievements of that game, none of this data is shown publicly with your username unless you enable your public profile.

### Public profiles, search and following

Profiles are private by default. If you enable **Public profile** in Account, your profile may show your username, bio, avatar, the public catalogue games you played most (hours and completion status), your verified and unlocked achievements in those games, public games you make, and up to 10 recommendations with comments. The bio allows up to 160 characters and comments up to 280; both are checked for inappropriate terms. Achievements hidden from the viewer appear without a name, description or image; games you hide from your profile show no hours or achievements. A recommendation for that game remains visible. Only games still in the public catalogue are included; external games, private betas and retired games are not. Your reward avatar may remain visible when you hide the game that granted it, but the game's name is not shown.

Search uses the beginning of your username and returns public profiles only. Following also requires a public profile. We store who follows whom and when, to show lists, counts and profiles with changes since your last visit; counts are public, only you can see your follower list, and your list of followed profiles is public. You can unfollow profiles or remove a follower; that person can follow you again. There is no feed or follow notification. Search is limited to 60 queries per minute and follows to 200 per day. To enforce the search limit, a salted hash of your account or IP address, as applicable, is temporarily stored in cache and expires after 60 seconds.

If you make your profile private again, it no longer appears in search or public lists; your followers are retained. The web profile asks search engines not to index it using `noindex`. Achievement rarity is shown when there are enough players to calculate it.

If you report a profile, we store your account, the reported profile, whether the report concerns the bio, recommendations or avatar, your optional message (up to 2,000 characters), and the date so the moderation team can review it. You can submit up to 10 reports per hour. The report stays in the moderation queue; if its sender deletes their account, it remains without a link to the reporter. Reports about a profile are deleted when the reported account is deleted.

Global avatars are uploaded by the project team. Makers can submit up to five avatars per game as rewards for completing the game or earning achievements. Square PNG or WebP images from 128 to 512 pixels and up to 512 KB are accepted; maker-submitted avatars must be approved before they can be equipped. Only verified achievements count. A reward may be granted retroactively to players who already meet its condition; if an achievement that qualified a player is revoked, the avatar is removed. Personal avatar uploads are not available. Approved images may appear on public profiles, in search and in lists of followed profiles.

### Statistics for makers

The maker of a game and the members of their team in the portal see aggregated, anonymous statistics for that game: players with playtime, total and median hours, the percentage who finish it, average rating and its distribution, players, hours and completed playthroughs by version, and weekly charts for the last 90 days with downloads, active players and hours. Downloads remain anonymous, as before. Names, accounts and data about any individual player are never shown. Each player-based figure is hidden when fewer than 5 players are behind it, and the same applies to each week and each version. Playtime, completed playthroughs and ratings exist only for players with an account, and playtime is measured in the desktop launcher. Testers of private betas are also included, with the same minimum of 5 players.

### On Android

- **Background downloads.** Downloads continue with the screen off through a foreground service that shows a notification with the progress. The notification permission is used only for that.

### Discord Rich Presence

Disabled by default. If enabled, the launcher sends the game's name, session start time, a compatible public catalogue image and public links to the game and launcher distribution to the desktop Discord client through local IPC. Discord publishes the activity according to your privacy settings and may fetch images from their public URLs. Save files, local paths, personal covers, launch arguments and credentials are not sent. No bot, token or Discord sign-in through the launcher is required. You can disable it and exclude games in Settings. Test builds with `review-` identifiers are never shared. Closing the launcher ends this integration, even if the game remains open.

Private betas also require the maker's explicit permission, disabled by default. While a private beta is running, the launcher checks the backend's authenticated catalogue at startup and approximately every minute to confirm that permission, even when the player has presence disabled. If permission cannot be confirmed, the beta is not published. Detecting revocation may take approximately one minute plus the request duration. The player token is sent only to the backend, never to Discord.

### Videos and external services

Game pages may include YouTube videos. In the launcher, hovering over or keyboard-focusing a video's play control preloads the YouTube player and API; this contacts YouTube and shares basic data before you press play. The video does not start until you activate the control. On the public website, the player loads only when you press play. The embedded player (`youtube-nocookie.com`) is subject to Google's [Privacy Policy](https://policies.google.com/privacy). The catalogue server contacts IGDB/Twitch only when a maker uses the optional metadata import, never on behalf of players.

### Google access

- The app requests only the `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.appdata` scope. It grants access to a private application folder in your Drive. **It cannot see or modify your other Drive files.**
- Data travels directly between your device and Google Drive. It does not pass through any project server, and we do not receive, store or share it.
- The access token is kept in your operating system's secure credential store. On Android it is stored encrypted with a key from the Android Keystore, in the app's private storage.
- Use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the [Google API Services User Data Policy](https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy), including the Limited Use requirements.

### Control

- You can disconnect your account at any time from **Settings** in the launcher. This revokes access and deletes the local token.
- You can also revoke access at <https://myaccount.google.com/permissions> and delete the app's data from Google Drive settings.

